A||||21||Formula88||88 suspension upgrade advice||||5||
Z||000000||Formula88||07-05-2001||02:45 PM||||Ok, my Formula rides like a truck. Obviously, the suspension is just worn out.  So here's what I'm thinking of doing.  Let me know what you think of this combo:<P>Fiero Store lowering springs<BR>Poly bushings front and rear (control arms, trailing links, sway bar links, etc)<BR>KYB HD shocks Front<BR>KYB struts Rear (HD if I can find them)<BR>Right now I'm going to be running BFG Comp TA's in the stock sizes or I may up to some Dunlop SP8000s in 245/50-15 Rear and 225/50-15 Front on the factory rims.<P>Plans for 17" rims down the road, but that likely won't be for a while.  So does this sound like a good setup, or am I making an expensive mistake?  I've not seen the KYB HD struts for the rear, so would I be better off with the regular KYB all the way around or with the HD up front.  The Fiero store has the HD fronts on closeout for only $10 more than the standard.  Also, with the lowering springs, etc., would I need to tweak the wheel alignment settings, or just go with a factory spec alignment?<P>I'm also thinking about poly engine, tranny, and dogbone mounts.  Any thoughts there?||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||07-05-2001||03:00 PM||||Rides like a truck? Have you driven and earlier year fiero's yet? Those modifications will definitely improve your handling but the ride quality will suffer. I decided to keep the stock springs and go with regular KYB shocks and struts. I also have Polly everything with stock size Cooper Cobra tires.  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||07-05-2001||03:23 PM||||If you don't want your Formula to ride like a truck, I would not do any of those suspension mods.  <P>I would get Monroe shocks/struts or if your a big spender adj. Koni's.  Lowering springs will really hurt the ride.  <P>I have pretty much everything you listed but lowering springs.  It rides like crap but it handles great.  Remember, we have Fiero's not Buicks....||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||07-05-2001||03:34 PM||||Handling and ride together can be done, but it's expensive.<P>Instead of poly front control arm bushings, use West Coast Fieros' Delrin bushings.<BR>I don't think that polyurethane is well suited to the kind of motions the rear suspension pivots make. If I were you, I'd replace the rear bushings with rubber or go straight to rod-ends.<BR>Instead of lowering springs (much stiffer than stock) keep your stock springs and install lowering spindles.<P>Talk to Slammed Fiero about shocks.<BR>I just started a thread about how different tire sizes front and rear work with weight distribution.<P>I'm running a rod-end dogbone and like it. It's no harsher cruising down the road, but it puts a little more engine vibration/noise into the cockpit under hard acceleration -&gt; enjoyable but certainly not overbearing.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||07-05-2001||04:39 PM||||I would avoid the stiff KYB shocks (monotube) and stick with the GR-2's.  also, I would avoid poly front bushings with the lowered springs......stay with the stock rubber (but replace them with new).  <P>I have had several '88s and this is the set-up I used with great success......both in handling and reasonably compliant ride:<P>-1" lowered springs (Fiero Store)<P>-KYB GR-2 shocks & struts<P>-poly rear bushings<P>-stock rubber front bushings<P>-poly sway bar end link bushings<P>-poly rear sway bar mount bushings<P>-new upper & lower balljoints<P>-performance tires (stock sizes)<P><BR>This set up gave me a great handling car tha was still "soft" enough not to be constantly pounding me into submission on city streets.<P>If you want to use poly up front and the stiffer KYB shocks, then avoid the lowered springs and buy lowered spindles instead.  I had this set-up on a Formula and the ride was horrible and it actually handled worse overall since the suspension was too still to absorb any irregularities/bumps in the road.  I would still use the lowered rear springs, etc.<P>I would also avoid putting on 225/50 & 245/50 tires on the stock wheels.......they are too big and won't do you any good.  I think it looks bad to......I did this very same thing 6 or 7 years ago and hated it.<P><BR>Whatever way you go.....good luck with it. <BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||AkursedX||07-05-2001||04:57 PM||||Well I have that exact setup on my 88GT, but with 17" rims.  <P>It is a rough ride, but the handling is phenominal!!  But, I would go with the KYB GR2's in the front.  My front end is almost too stiff for the rest of the car, and I think the GR2's would help this out.  <P>But, you will have a great handling car with that setup and a good set of rubber.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||07-05-2001||09:31 PM||||I think you misunderstood me.  The bad ride is from a shot suspension.  I like a firm ride, but this has no dampening at all hardly.  And that's a poor, rough ride compared to how a Formula is SUPPOSED to ride.  I'm comparing it to LZeitgeists 60K mile original Formula.  His handles much better, rides much better, and has a smoother ride.  I'm not looking for Buick handling.  I'm looking for the performance the car should have, plus a bit more.  So maybe use the standard KYB's all the way round instead of the HD fronts.  Has anyone had any experience with West Coast's Delrin bushings?  Are they worth the expense?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||07-06-2001||10:07 AM||||I have Delrin bushings I made myself on the front of my '87 GT. I think that they are hardly any harsher for ride quality and NVH than the stock rubber bushings. However, they do not bind like polyurethane, and let the suspension move freely like it should. The are also harder than polyurethane.<BR>Whether that's worth the expense to you I can't answer.||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||07-06-2001||10:15 AM||||The coilovers, and if you want, Spherical Bearing (ie, Ferrari F50) rear links, etc. Can be had from Held Motorsports.<P>They make great stuff: <A HREF="http://www.heldmotorsports.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.heldmotorsports.com</A> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||montage||07-06-2001||12:07 PM||||I just received new Dunlop 8000 245 50 15 rear and 225 50 15 front and they are great. More importantly, the stock wheel sizes are in the range recommended by Dunlop and the stock widths are the same Dunlop uses for tire measurements. Also here is a link showing that the 8000 is also a good tire.  <A HREF="http://integra.vtec.net/wheels/tiretest.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://integra.vtec.net/wheels/tiretest.htm</A> <BR>I just got through evaluating wheels and I was going to go to 17 but didn't for a couple of reasons. First most wheels are heavy here is a link for some weights, <A HREF="http://www.miata.net/faq/wheel_weights.html#weight" TARGET=_blank>http://www.miata.net/faq/wheel_weights.html#weight</A>  , the only thing less than a stock wheel are forged racing wheels (SSR comps, Racing Hart, etc.) so unless you go big dollars the added weight in my opinion offsets any gains from lower profiles (which may also be a problem). Most stock wheels (high pressure cast) are fairly light weight so stick to those unless you go with forged, most after market wheels are for looks and are conventionally casts so they are heavy. I weighed my stock wheels and the front are 20 lbs the rear are 19 lbs (yes this is correct). The Dunlop tires are 22 and 25 lbs compared to the stock tires, which are 18 and 20 lbs. The other reason is most racers do not use low profile tires for two basic reasons, first you get no warning if the tires break loose and secondly as you corner with a low profile tire you loose tread contact patch area. So I figured these 50 series Dunlop's have about the right side wall height (profile is a percentage must calculate the actual sidewall height) that most race tires are. Additionally, the front tires are 0.7" smaller than stock and the rears are 0.4" smaller than stock so this gives a slightly higher effective gear ratio (improves 0-60 times) and transfers a little more weight to the front hopefully improving handling. <P>I am also rebuilding the front and just replacing the upper bushings with stock rubber. I was told with the Fiero to not put poly the front it is too stiff (front is light already) and I think the difference between poly and old bushings is great but not so sure compared to new rubber. Not to mention the fit problems people have, you have to burn the bushings out which would affect the heat treatment of the A arms, and the new rubber is cold formed to the sleeves where poly is not.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||07-06-2001||02:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by montage:<BR><B> Not to mention the fit problems people have, you have to burn the bushings out which would affect the heat treatment of the A arms, and the new rubber is cold formed to the sleeves where poly is not.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>&lt;chuckle&gt;<P>the control arms are stamped and welded, but not heat treated as far as I know.||||reg||1
Z||000011||montage||07-06-2001||04:21 PM||||If they are not heat treated then I would be worried about warping from the heat especially given the fact that they are stamped (which I knew but was not sure if they were heat treated). The stamping would create residual stresses in the part so if heated in a localized area this could slightly distort that area and the high residual stresses would magnify this affect. Don't get me wrong that there would be a gross distortion, however a small distortion is very possible. <P>It would be interesting if anyone has directly compared poly to new rubber? May be skid pad test with the same tires? Any g-tech owners have g-force info?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Formula88||07-08-2001||01:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>The coilovers, and if you want, Spherical Bearing (ie, Ferrari F50) rear links, etc. Can be had from Held Motorsports.<P>They make great stuff: <A HREF="http://www.heldmotorsports.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.heldmotorsports.com</A> <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm not looking for that much. I just want to lower it a bit to help the looks, and improve on the factory handling some.  The firm ride doesn't bother me, but my dampening is so bad, the tires don't stay on the road over expansion joint in a turn.  <P>I've heard people talking about the Fiero Store lowering springs not holding up well over time and sagging.  Anyone have this problem?  If you've got them, how long have you had them.<P>I'm going with the full-goose-bozo Held setup for my kit car - which comes much later with much more money.  Right now I just want to play with my everyday driver Formula.||||reg||1
Z||000013||ray b||07-08-2001||12:18 PM||||poly bushings are made in diffrent weights,and canbe softer than stock rubber<BR>or much harder. but only hards are made for<BR>fiero's now??are the 88's poly the same hardness as pre-88 cars used .<BR>anyone know where to get softer poly for 88's?<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000014||RoadRocket||07-08-2001||04:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B><BR>&lt;snip&gt; <P>-1" lowered springs (Fiero Store)<P>-KYB GR-2 shocks & struts<P>-poly rear bushings<P>-stock rubber front bushings<P>-poly sway bar end link bushings<P>-poly rear sway bar mount bushings<P>-new upper & lower balljoints<P>-performance tires (stock sizes)<P><BR>This set up gave me a great handling car tha was still "soft" enough not to be constantly pounding me into submission on city streets.<BR></B><BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>But what does that cost? Parts and Labor? I just had my Fiero looked over yesterday and it wasn't cheap. Like Formula88, I'd like to do an upgrade, but I don't have the time to do it myself.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||mrfiero||07-08-2001||05:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RoadRocket:<BR><B> But what does that cost? Parts and Labor? I just had my Fiero looked over yesterday and it wasn't cheap. Like Formula88, I'd like to do an upgrade, but I don't have the time to do it myself.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OK......I do all of my own work, so labor costs don't factor into this (at least for me).  Obviously, if you had a shop install everything it would add up fast.<P>Here's what I paid:<P>-1" lowered springs.....$250<P>-KYB shocks & struts.....$150<P>-poly rear bushings.....$75<P>-stock rubber front bushings.....$100<P>-poly end link bushungs.....$30<P>-poly sway bar bushings.....$15<P>-new upper & lower balljoints.....$200<P>-performance tires.....$300<P>That totals $1120.......not cheap, but you won't have to mess with anything for a long, long time.  I am also replacing the rack and inner & outer tie rods, so the entire suspension on my GT will be brand new. <P>You don't have to buy the lowered springs or performance tires (if your tires are still OK)......same thing with the poly end links, etc.<P>If you were to replace your shocks & struts as well as the bushings your Fiero will drive like it was new.  <P>I am considering selling rebuilt control arms, rear links, etc to save people the hassles and time for doing it themselves.  It would include new balljoints and new bushings (either poly or rubber) as well as being repainted, etc.  <P>Also, for an additional fee I'll deliver and install the parts.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||RoadRocket||07-09-2001||12:30 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B>That totals $1120.......not cheap, but you won't have to mess with anything for a long, long time.  I am also replacing the rack and inner & outer tie rods, so the entire suspension on my GT will be brand new. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Youch! <P>A little more than I wanna pay.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>Of course, the mechanic quoted me about 600. But I think that was to replace the shocks and struts only. <P>Of course, I knew I was getting into a bit of a money pit when I resurrected my Fiero. I guess I'll bite the bullet, eventually.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>--<BR>Ed Dana<BR>88 Coupe.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Black88GT||07-09-2001||12:53 AM||||I think the Fiero Store springs lower more then 1", like 1.5".  Eibachs are 1" sold at <A HREF="http://www.americancustomeng.com." TARGET=_blank>www.americancustomeng.com.</A>   I have heard to buy the Fiero Stores because the Eibachs don't lower enough.<P>I am going to get lowering springs installed shortly (found a set for 100 bucks).  I can handle shocks/bushings but struts/springs are out of my league.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||mrfiero||07-09-2001||01:14 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RoadRocket:<BR><B> Youch! <P>A little more than I wanna pay.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>Of course, the mechanic quoted me about 600. But I think that was to replace the shocks and struts only. <P>Of course, I knew I was getting into a bit of a money pit when I resurrected my Fiero. I guess I'll bite the bullet, eventually.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Double youch!  $600 for shocks & struts?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>I hope that includes a good 4 wheel alignment.<P>BTW......I wasn't kidding, I would be more than happy to take a trip to Arizona one weekend and  install that stuff for you (or you could drive up to Denver).<P>For that same $600 you could get everything I listed except the lowered springs and performance tires! <P>LMK.||||reg||1
Z||000019||mrfiero||07-09-2001||01:20 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B><BR>I am going to get lowering springs installed shortly (found a set for 100 bucks).  I can handle shocks/bushings but struts/springs are out of my league.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Jeff,<P>Springs and struts really aren't that hard to do.  Anyone with common hand tools can accomplish it in a day.   <P>Also, when doing the springs it's a great time to do the lower balljoints (since it'll be apart).<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||RoadRocket||07-10-2001||12:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B> <BR>Double youch!  $600 for shocks & struts?     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>I hope that includes a good 4 wheel alignment.<P>BTW......I wasn't kidding, I would be more than happy to take a trip to Arizona one weekend and  install that stuff for you (or you could drive up to Denver).<P>For that same $600 you could get everything I listed except the lowered springs and performance tires! <P>LMK.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I already got new tires.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Springs, I'm not too concerned about. I just want the car to drive like it did when it was new.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><P>I might take you up on your offer, but it looks like I got prioritize for right now. My Intrepid needs some work to get it ready for a long trip. <P>Gotta take care of that first...<BR><P>------------------<BR>--<BR>Ed Dana<BR>88 Coupe.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Formula88||11-15-2001||06:15 PM||||Well, I finally got my KYB's installed.  How's that for procrastination!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Anyway, I went with the Gas-A-Just front and GR-2 rear.  Nice.  No other changes to the suspension, yet.  But it rides soooo much better now.  Still firm, as it should be, but you don't rattle your fillings loose over highway expansion joints.<P>Turns out, I still had my ORIGINAL shocks and struts.  The struts were so worn out, I could compress them with my bare hands and they stayed compressed when I let go.  No to mention, how easy it was to compress them.  the fronts were just as bad.||||reg||1
